{{also|absént}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|abs.}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₁es-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|absent}}, from {{der|en|frm|absent}}, from {{der|en|fro|ausent|}}, and their source, {{der|en|la|absens|}}, present participle of {{m|la|absum||to be away from}}, from {{m|la|ab||away}} + {{m|la|sum||to be}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.sn̩t/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.sn̩t/|a=US}}, {{enPR|ăb'sənt}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-absent.ogg|a=US}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}<ref>{{R:MW3 1976|page=6}}</ref>

# {{lb|en|not comparable}} Being [[away]] from a place; [[withdrawn]] from a place; existing but not [[present]]; {{lb|en|sometimes}} [[missing#Adjective|missing]]. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=8}}</ref>
#: {{ant|en|present#Adjective}}
#: {{cot|en|accounted for<!--Those "[either] present or accounted for" = "present" + "absent but accounted for"; those plus the ones "absent and not accounted for" = everyone.-->}}
#: {{ux|en|Owing to his own illness and then his family's, Ramzi has often been '''absent''' from class this month. We will help him catch up with his studies.}}
#: {{ux|en|When they were able to return to the clinic some months later, certain pieces of equipment were '''absent'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1623|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=All’s Well That Ends Well|section=II-iii|passage=Expecting '''absent''' friends.}}
# {{lb|en|not comparable}} Not [[exist]]ing. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ant|en|present#Adjective|existing#Adjective|extant#Adjective}}
#: {{hypo|en|lacking#Adjective<qq:denotes that the absent thing [[ought to]] exist>}}
#: {{ux|en|The body part was rudimentary or '''absent''' in 1% of specimens.}}
#: {{ux|en|Empathy seemed to be '''absent''' from the messages.<!--It was likewise [[lacking]], because it was desirable.-->}}
#: {{ux|en|Signs of forced entry were '''absent'''.<!--They were not "lacking", because "lacking" would imply that forced entry is desirable.-->}}
# {{lb|en|comparable}} [[inattentive|Inattentive]] to what is passing; [[absent-minded]]; [[preoccupied]]. {{defdate|First attested in the early 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ant|en|present#Adjective}}
#: {{ux|en|Tom was there, but he seemed '''absent''' and withdrawn. Normally he is quite present [&equals; engaged] during a meeting.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1746-1747|author=Chesterfield|title=Letters to his Son|passage=What is commonly called an '''absent''' man is commonly either a very weak or a very affected man.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=For days Ailie had an '''absent''' eye and a sad face, and it so fell out that in all that time young Heriotside, who had scarce missed a day, was laid up with a broken arm and never came near her.}}

